<title>USA: Rocket launches navigation satellite.</title>
<headline>Rocket launches navigation satellite.</headline>
<dateline>CAPE CANAVERAL, Fla. 1996-09-12</dateline>
<text>
<p>A McDonnell Douglas Corp Delta 2 rocket thundered into orbit on Thursday carrying a $55 million U.S. Air Force navigation satellite.</p>
<p>The 12-story-tall Delta 2 lifted off its launch pad at Cape Canaveral Air Station, Florida, at 4:49 a.m. EDT (0849 GMT).</p>
<p>The satellite, built by Rockwell International, was placed into a 12,000 mile (19,300 km) circular orbit about 25 minutes later. It is the second of a new generation of Global Positioning System satellites and will replace an ageing satellite in orbit.</p>
<p>The 24-satellite system allows military users to determine their position anywhere on earth to an accuracy of 30 feet and permits civilians, such as mariners, to calculate their location to within 300 feet (90 metres).</p>
</text>
